WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:06.200
Tap the tendon below your kneecap, and your leg kicks.

2
00:00:07.100 --> 00:00:09.620
What happens to the muscle on the other side of the joint?

3
00:00:10.683 --> 00:00:12.563
The tap stretches the quadriceps.

4
00:00:13.283 --> 00:00:16.103
Inside it lie stretch sensors called muscle spindles.

5
00:00:17.067 --> 00:00:20.107
A spindle lies alongside the force-making fibres.

6
00:00:21.047 --> 00:00:23.447
It lengthens with the muscle, so it reports stretch.

7
00:00:24.500 --> 00:00:28.360
Its sensory axons, or afferents, carry signals into the cord.

8
00:00:29.260 --> 00:00:30.900
The main ones are called Ia fibres.

9
00:00:31.850 --> 00:00:33.190
The tap makes them fire.

10
00:00:34.110 --> 00:00:36.650
They enter the cord through the dorsal root, and they branch.

11
00:00:37.733 --> 00:00:41.253
One branch excites the quadriceps motor neurons directly.

12
00:00:41.953 --> 00:00:43.153
That is one synapse.

13
00:00:43.893 --> 00:00:45.453
The pool goes up, and the leg kicks.

14
00:00:49.633 --> 00:00:54.013
But the motor neurons of the hamstrings, on the back of the thigh, are held back.

15
00:00:54.893 --> 00:00:56.693
A Ia fibre only ever excites.

16
00:00:57.553 --> 00:00:58.833
How can it hold anything back?

17
00:01:04.783 --> 00:01:09.543
Its second branch excites an inhibitory interneuron, on the same side of the cord.

18
00:01:10.567 --> 00:01:13.747
That interneuron inhibits the hamstring motor neurons.

19
00:01:14.687 --> 00:01:15.687
Their pool goes down.

20
00:01:16.527 --> 00:01:17.787
This path has two synapses.

21
00:01:18.900 --> 00:01:24.000
In this pathway, the excitatory Ia fibre inhibits the antagonist through an

22
00:01:24.000 --> 00:01:25.240
inhibitory interneuron.

23
00:01:26.250 --> 00:01:28.670
One stretch, two commands, to two pools.

24
00:01:29.710 --> 00:01:32.010
Sherrington called this pairing reciprocal innervation.

25
00:01:33.133 --> 00:01:35.953
A second sensor shows why the spindle needs help.

26
00:01:36.753 --> 00:01:39.493
The Golgi tendon organ sits where muscle meets tendon.

27
00:01:40.533 --> 00:01:44.333
Each one lies in line with a few fibres and senses their pull.

28
00:01:45.233 --> 00:01:47.193
Together they signal force, not length.

29
00:01:48.467 --> 00:01:52.467
A unit pulling on one tendon organ twitches, so that organ fires.

30
00:01:53.387 --> 00:01:55.727
The shortened spindle can fall slack and go quiet.

31
00:01:56.750 --> 00:01:58.590
That slack spindle is a problem.

32
00:01:59.730 --> 00:02:00.890
Gamma motor neurons fix it.

33
00:02:01.783 --> 00:02:05.223
They supply only the spindle, and they contract its two ends.

34
00:02:06.183 --> 00:02:10.483
That pulls the centre of the spindle taut again, where the Ia endings wrap.

35
00:02:11.483 --> 00:02:15.143
Think of a tape measure that winds itself in, so it never goes loose.

36
00:02:16.216 --> 00:02:21.056
When alpha and gamma motor neurons fire together, the spindle can stay in range

37
00:02:21.056 --> 00:02:22.056
during a contraction.

38
00:02:23.050 --> 00:02:25.250
So ask the two questions of any reflex.

39
00:02:26.170 --> 00:02:30.050
A stretch pushes its own muscle's pool up and its antagonist's pool down.

40
00:02:31.000 --> 00:02:34.820
And gamma drive keeps the spindle signalling while the muscle shortens.
